<p>An <strong>underpaid insurance claim in Port Arthur</strong> rarely looks like a dramatic refusal. More often it is a check that arrives on time but covers only part of the work, an estimate that leaves out rooms or a roof slope, or a depreciation holdback that leaves the homeowner unable to start repairs. Because the claim was technically paid, many people assume there is nothing left to do. That is not necessarily true. When a settlement does not match the real cost to repair, the reasons can usually be traced, documented and presented back to the insurer.</p>

<h2>Signs Your Claim May Be Underpaid</h2>
<ul>
<li>Licensed contractors’ bids are well above the insurer’s estimate for the same work.</li>
<li>The estimate repairs part of a roof slope or wall when the materials cannot be matched or patched.</li>
<li>Interior rooms with visible staining, or the attic and insulation, are missing.</li>
<li>Fences, sheds, carports or gutters are left off.</li>
<li>Permit costs, code-required items or TDI windstorm requirements are not addressed.</li>
<li>Depreciation looks unusually high for the age and condition of the materials.</li>
<li>Contents were estimated as a lump sum or not at all.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where the Gap Usually Comes From</h2>
<table>
