<h2>Why Underpayment Is Common After Port Arthur Storms</h2>
<p>After a hurricane or major wind event, Southeast Texas sees a surge in claims at the same time that labor and materials become scarce. Estimates written in the first weeks may not reflect what contractors are actually charging months later when repairs begin. Many Jefferson County homes also carry separate TWIA wind and homeowners policies, so the loss may be split across two estimates that each leave out something the other assumed was covered. And percentage-based hurricane deductibles mean a few missing line items can be the difference between a payment and no payment at all. Our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> shows how the numbers interact.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Replacement Cost, Actual Cash Value and Holdbacks</h2>
<p>Under many replacement cost policies, the insurer first pays actual cash value: the cost to replace minus depreciation. The withheld depreciation is released after repairs are completed and documented, often within a deadline stated in the policy. If you never submit for that holdback, it may never be paid. Some Texas policies cover roofs only at actual cash value or use a roof payment schedule based on age, which changes the math entirely. Read the declarations and roof endorsements before assuming what the policy owes.</p>

<h2>Contents and Living Expenses Are Often Underpaid Too</h2>
<p>Underpayment is not limited to the building. Contents claims are frequently settled from a quick list or a rough percentage of the dwelling limit, which can leave out entire rooms of belongings. A detailed inventory with descriptions, quantities, approximate ages and current replacement prices usually supports a much more accurate figure. Additional living expense claims can also fall short when receipts are missing or the insurer limits the time it expects repairs to take. If the home stays unlivable because repairs are delayed by contractor availability after a regional storm, that context belongs in the claim.</p>

<h2>Small Gaps Add Up</h2>
<p>One missing line item rarely looks significant. But a roof estimate that leaves out drip edge, starter strip, a steep-pitch charge and a few squares of waste, combined with an interior estimate that paints one wall instead of a whole room and a missing fence section, can add up to a meaningful difference. With a percentage hurricane deductible, those gaps may decide whether there is any payment at all. That is why reviewing every page of the estimate, not just the total, matters.</p>

<h2>Our Process for Underpaid Claims</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Collect the paperwork.</strong> Your policy, every estimate and payment letter, photos and any contractor bids.</li>
<li><strong>Re-inspect the property.</strong> Document the roof, exterior, attic, interior and other structures, including damage the first estimate missed.</li>
<li><strong>Build an independent estimate.</strong> A line-item Xactimate estimate allows a direct comparison with the carrier’s figures.</li>
<li><strong>Present a supplement.</strong> We send the insurer the differences with supporting photos and explanations. See <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Follow through.</strong> We track responses and keep communications in writing. If the insurer agrees on coverage but not amount, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> may be available.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Why Work With a Port Arthur Public Adjuster</h2>
<p>A Port Arthur Public Adjuster represents the policyholder only. Our job on an underpaid claim is to find out whether the payment matches the policy and the damage, and if it does not, to show the insurer specifically why. Sometimes the review finds the payment was reasonable, and we will say so. Other times it uncovers missed areas, incorrect deductibles or depreciation that was never claimed. We cannot promise any particular increase.</p>
<p>It is also not necessarily too late if you already accepted a payment; our article on <a href="/when-is-it-too-late-to-hire-a-public-adjuster/">when it is too late to hire a public adjuster</a> covers timing. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Start with a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call [[phone]].</p>
